A||N||18||fropuff||A question for the legal experts..||||1||
Z||000000||fropuff||08-26-2001||11:54 PM||||Hey guys.  On Thursday, I was involved in an accident which demolished the rear end of my 86 GT.  What happened was this: I was backing out of my parking spot while turning to the left.  I checked my right blind spot, and no one was there.  So I started pulling out; very slowly.  (1-2 MPH)  I quickly checked the front and my left blind spot.  In that short time (1-1.5 seconds), a van came flying through the lot and hit my right rear corner.  He had to be going pretty fast for me to not see him, as there is a long straightaway.  He took out part of the fastback clip, the rear fascia, and the right tail lamp assembly; also sliced through all the wires for the rear lamps.  There's around $3000-$5000 damage, including all the new parts.  Now, I thought it'd be my fault, since he had the right-of-way.  However, he had to be going ~30-35 MPH to do that much damage.  He also said he was in a hurry, so I had to let him go.  He said he would take care of his damage himself, I'd take care of mine.  He did paint work previously, so he'd fix his.  (I put a 6" by 12" scrape on his new van; easily buffed; no dent)  Why would he do it himself if he has full coverage?  Hiding something maybe??  I did get his name and number though.  After that, we went to the dealer where he two weeks prior got his van, under his wife's name.  The number there was different than the one he gave me.  Hmm.  The cop called tonight, and he said that he's sending the papers to me.  He got the guy's info, as he wasn't there when the cop got there.  He said the guy's license was revoked, so he's getting a citation for that.  Now my question.. could I get paid from his insurance company?  He has full coverage; I have liability.  I'm going to get the info tomorrow, so I'll get back.  I appreciate any help.     <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierohoho||08-27-2001||12:13 AM||||Call your insurance company.<P>The way it should work is, he was revoked and if he wasn't driving illegally there would have been no accident therefore it was his fault. <P>When you get the police report it will show he was cited and then your insurance company should go after his to get the repair money.<P>See if you can get your insurance to go after his company if he had one, they may be able to get them to pay up then they cancel him.<P>Time for me to preach: If you have an accident no matter how small, write down the license plate and vehicle description, get the drivers full name, (first,middle,last), and their date of birth, and their drivers license number. <P>If you have time write down the VIN number from the other vehicle. If the other person is in too much of a hurry call the cops asap, it's been my experience they usually have something to hide, if they leave tell the cops which way they went and hope for the best and as always never admit the accident was your fault, sorry, Amen. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pennockpic.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||08-27-2001||12:25 AM||||I never said whose fault it was; I was too busy swearing and throwing stuff.  The reason we went to the dealer was because he had the temporary plate on it.  I know a lot about cars, so I had a great description ('01 Dodge fullsize cargo van, 15-passenger, medium red/burgundy, with a roll of carpet in the back; as well as a scrape on the right side, in front of the rear wheel)  All I know is that he did not want to be around.  I typed in the number he gave me on the web, and it came up with a different name.  It took him quite a while to write the number down too.  We're thinking of getting some estimates on the repair, for new rear panels and an entire paint job, and bring it to his insurance company and tell them to pay it.  His wife had insurance, which is why the van was under her name.  Thanks again.        ||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||08-27-2001||12:54 AM||||was it a private parking lot?? not a public road/right of way? that may make a big diff to the ins co.<BR>are you in a no fault ins state?? trucks may be diff in the laws vs cars<BR>every state has diff laws esp if off road.<BR>so ask a local cop or lawyer.<BR>don't beleave a ins co rep if he says no check the law.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000004||fropuff||08-27-2001||12:57 AM||||Yep, it was a private gas station parking lot.  I have no idea if MN is a no-fault state, nor if vans are treated differently.  I'll have to see what the cop says.  Thanks. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||RossT||08-27-2001||12:59 AM||||The law finds the fault of "backing up accidents" most of the time, as that of the person doing the backing up cause you are responsible for checking for traffic, but since there was high speeds on his part you might have a case against him.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||08-27-2001||01:00 AM||||sorry to say when you back up, 9/10 of the time your the one accepting responsibility. There are possibilities if you have witnesses that can say the other driver was doing something extraordinary like going 70mph in a parking lot or DUI. It dont matter if he had licence or whatever. Usually in parking lots police wont even come to the scene unless there is injury or reckless operation involved. I think youll find your just SOL. Unless you have collision coverage to fix your car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroKyle||08-27-2001||01:03 AM||||Only thing i would be worried about is the cheap ass insurance companies totalling your Fiero with that kind of a damage estimate.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||08-27-2001||05:55 AM||||Backing up will normally be your fault, but...if he was cited for something, you might have an edge.  Also, try to find witnesses that will testify that he was travelling at a high rate of speed.  Private vs. public property won't matter to the insurance company.  You may have to hire an attorney, but you stand a good chance of winning.  BTW--If the insurance company decides to settle with you and there really is $3000-$5000 worth of damage, they <B>will</B> total your car.  The good news is that you should be able to buy the car back for something like 10% of the totalled amount (at least that's what it is in Indiana).  If you go that route, do some of the work yourself, buy some used parts, and you should come out at least even.||||reg||1
Z||000009||thomas_l||08-27-2001||09:06 AM||||Chances are, he has no insurance. The policy on that van (and its title too) are probably all solely in his wife's name because he is not licensed to drive period.<P>Hopefully you have uninsured motorist coverage. Then your own insurance company fixes your car and they go off and sue the luser that hit you. If you don't have it then you sue him in small claims and hope his wife isn't a deadbeat like him and you actually see money after you get the judgement.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||08-27-2001||11:05 AM||||the speed limit in a parking lot in almost all states is 15mph.<P> that is because of situations exactly like yours -when you are backing out, esp if you are next to a large vehicle, you may not be able to see.<P> so if another car is coming and its going 15mph or less, it will have time to either sound its horn, go around you, or stop - this guy did none of the three - if he was going fast enough to do all that damage he didnt have time to do anything.<P>  the only way any logical person would place the blame on you for this accident would be if you put the car in reverse and pulled out fast and blind.<P> 1. Get a lawyer.<BR> 2. Sue him and the owner of the vehicle.<BR> 3. File a complant against him for operating without a license.<BR> 4. sue him somemore.<BR> 5. If his lawyer starts to balk, complain about your neck and back.||||reg||11
Z||000011||Ryan Wright||08-27-2001||11:39 AM||||If you haven't already done so,<P>CALL YOUR INSURANCE COMPANY!<P>They will help you sort this mess out. I am going through a similar situation right now where I was run off the road by another driver who passed me, cut me off & slammed on his brakes (trying to force me to rear-end him). I didn't rear-end him but I totalled my car & knocked over a city light pole. The guy fled the scene but the cops treated him like a witness and said it was a 1 car accident. Idiots. Anyway, my insurance company is suing him for me. Saves me the hassle of a lawyer.<P>If you have uninsured motorist coverage (You should, it's only a couple of bucks per month. If you don't, WHY NOT?!) your insurance will pay for your vehicle. They also may very well sue the other driver for you. If you don't have it, you're out of luck, however: A good insurance agent will give you all sorts of advice and help you out regardless. So, first, call or visit your agent and explain everything to them. Find out what your options are.<P>Finally, if all else fails, sue this prick. It's going to look really bad on his part, driving with a revoked license... With that sort of a record I'm sure this isn't the first time he's done something like this.<P>EDIT: Next time, call the cops. ALWAYS, ALWAYS call the cops. They would have arrested this SOB on the spot for driving with a revoked license, no insurance, possibly speeding... If you would have called them, he would have run, and they would have hopefully chased him down. Lastly, always write down the license plate number. Looks like you did your homework by writing down the info from the temporary plates; that's good. But next time, call the cops regardless of what anybody says. I don't go easy on people. "Please just let me pay for it myself, I don't want to deal with my insurance company" usually means "Please just let me go so I can screw you, I don't want to take responsibility for this, and I'm damn sure not going to give you a penny." The cops will come and do their thing, and then you go to their insurance company whether they like it or not.<P>------------------<BR>-Ryan Wright (http://www.ryanwright.com)<p>[This message has been edited by Ryan Wright (edited 08-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cooter||08-27-2001||03:31 PM||||With him fleeing the scene of an accident, you should be able to burn his butt. I would go for criminal charges now and civil charges later, when he is found.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fropuff||08-27-2001||06:36 PM||||He was found; that's why the cops found out he had a revoked license.  He sure wasn't going 15 MPH.  If he was, he wouldn't have done that much damage, and I would have seen him.  The first thing he said was:  "Where did you come from?"  So he didn't see me at all.  The police were called, but I wasn't aware of it.  I'm sure the other guy knew they would be, so he had to go..  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||08-27-2001||06:56 PM||||about 'uninsured motorist' insurance, in most states it only covers injuries and NOT property. You cant even get it for a car itself in Ohio. I think Michigan has no fault where your insurance company pays for fixing only your car, no matter who is at fault. You are always open to file a lawsuit against the driver and owner, it would be a civil action and would prob be a very long time before you got anything, if any at all. Years even. Most states will repair your car under your collision damage (if you have it) less your deductible and then sue the other party to recover what they paid you. Also usually any damage over $500 and up depending on how your company rates are figured will result in your rates being increased.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fropuff||08-27-2001||09:49 PM||||Well, I got the accident report, and here's what it showed:<P>-The name he gived me was incorrect.  He said Tony; his real name is Lawrence.<P>-The number he gave me was incorrect.<P>-He said I tried to "squeeze by him" in the lot, and we came into contact.  Whatever.<P>-He's getting cited for a revoked licence (Illinois) ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ken Wittlief||08-28-2001||11:19 AM||||get a lawyer<P>I know most people hate lawyers, but the legal system is the way it is, and lawyers are a part of it.<P> sometimes the toilet in my bathroom gets plugged, and I have to get the plunger out of the garage and deal with it, and clean up the mess.<P> I had a dead squirrel in the flu of my chimney this spring, - I had to put on the organic filter resperator and latex gloves to clean it out - the maggots had already gotten to it and it was not a pleasent thing to do.<P> Not to lump lawyers in with plugged up toilets and decayed dead animals, my point is sometimes you gotta deal with really Kwrappy stuff - you dont like it, its not pleasent - but your option is to eat several thousand dollars worth the damage some irresponsible dude done to your machine.<P>  Sic the lawyers on him - let them do their job - let them get their cut - take a deep breath, curse the system for the way it is<P> and then get on with your life.<P>  If you let this guy off the hook he is not gonna change his behaviour - next time he may kill someone.||||reg||10
Z||000017||stsmithgt||08-28-2001||11:36 AM||||LOL ...lawyers compared to plungers. That's a good one.||||reg||10
Z||000018||p-did||09-04-2001||11:17 PM||||Well, I got the estimate back.  $2783.10, including new rear fascia, tail lanses; used fastback clip.  Not as bad as I expected; I still don't know if they'll total it...<P>------------------<BR>Jay Olson<BR>(AKA "fropuff")<BR>'86 GT, smashed rear fascia, fastback clip, right tail lamp assembly||||reg||1
